For any class $\mathcal{X}$ of groups, define the following operators:
- $H(\mathcal{X})$ = the class of all homomorphic images of groups in $\mathcal{X}$;
- $S(\mathcal{X})$ = the class of all groups isomorphic to a subgroup of some group in $\mathcal{X}$;
- $P(\mathcal{X})$ = the class of all groups isomorphic to an (unrestricted) direct product $\prod_{i\in I} G_i$ of an arbitrary family $(G_i)_{i\in I}$ of groups in $\mathcal{X}$;
- $P_f(\mathcal{X})$ = the class of all groups isomorphic to a direct product of a finite family of groups in $\mathcal{X}$.

By Birkhoff's HSP theorem, $H(S(P(\mathcal{X})))$ is the variety of groups generated by $\mathcal{X}$. A group $G$ is called metabelian if its commutator subgroup $[G,G]$ is abelian (equivalently, if $G$ has an abelian normal subgroup with abelian quotient).

Question: Let $\mathcal{M}$ be any class of metabelian groups. Must the inclusion
$$H(S(P_f(\mathcal{M}))) \;\subseteq\; S(H(P(S(\mathcal{M}))))$$
hold?

